{
  "image_name": "c988b2b1bd5d7706900853eb0a71f9d3.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how sandy and loamy soils handle water under dry conditions and during a rainfall event in a forested landscape. Overall, it highlights that soil texture strongly changes where water is stored and how plants access it.\n\nIn dry conditions, the sandy soil shows deeper downward movement and recharge toward the groundwater table, with trees apparently using deep roots and hydraulic lift to move water upward. Evaporation and transpiration remove water from the surface and canopy. In contrast, the loamy soil retains more water higher in the profile, where roots can take it up more directly. Upward capillary rise from the groundwater table is emphasized in the loam, suggesting stronger movement of water back toward the root zone.\n\nDuring precipitation, both soils receive infiltration, but the patterns differ. Sandy soil appears to let water penetrate more deeply and redistribute through a larger volume, leading to broader subsurface wetting. Loamy soil seems to hold more water near the surface, with a shallower wetted zone around roots. The center boundary also indicates some surface run-off during rain. Taken together, the figure shows deeper drainage in sand versus stronger near-surface retention and upward supply in loam.",
